BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 148.0 Td (  In the dawn of the First Age, the Sky-Weavers Sun and Moon stood upon the threshold of existen) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 138.0 Td (    ce. Sun held the golden embers of creation, a finite gift meant to bridge the void. To withh) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 128.0 Td (    old was to remain stagnant; to share was the gamble of divinity. Sun cast the embers across ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 118.0 Td (    the abyss, tripling their warmth as they traveled, a gesture of absolute faith.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 108.0 Td (  ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 98.0 Td (  Moon, waiting in the velvet dark, caught the fire. Had Moon consumed the light, the world woul) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 88.0 Td (    d have frozen in selfish shadows, forever dim. Instead, Moon understood the covenant of the ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 78.0 Td (    cycle. By returning a portion of the fire back to Sun, the light grew brighter, turning the ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 68.0 Td (    void into a garden of stars. This was the Law of the Echo: what is given with open hands ret) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 58.0 Td (    urns with interest, and what is guarded with a clenched fist withers into ash. The heavens l) Tj ET
