BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 378.0 Td (      In the beginning, the Weaver held five seeds of silver. She could keep them, locked in her) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 368.0 Td (         iron fist, or cast them into the fertile void. She chose to cast them. As they fell, a ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 358.0 Td (        cosmic wind-the multiplier of the gods-tripled their count. The Gardener caught the fift) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 348.0 Td (        een golden grains, his palms cupped in expectation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 338.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 328.0 Td (      Greed is a hungry ghost, whispering of hidden caches and solitary wealth. But the Gardener) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 318.0 Td (         knew the ancient law of the scales: what is given must be returned, or the cycle breaks) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 308.0 Td (        . He planted the seeds, nurtured them with his own essence, and harvested their fruit. F) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 298.0 Td (        rom the fifteen, he kept a portion for his labor but returned a generous sum to the Weav) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 288.0 Td (        er's outstretched hand.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 278.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 268.0 Td (      The Weaver's store swelled; the Gardener's soil thrived. They played this dance through eo) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 258.0 Td (        ns, two souls tethered by the golden thread of trust. When a hand offered, a hand accept) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 248.0 Td (        ed, and a hand returned, the stars burned brighter. But when a heart hardened, fearing l) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 238.0 Td (        oss, the garden turned to ash. For the universe does not reward the hoarder, but the one) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 228.0 Td (         who understands that to hold everything is, in the end, to possess nothing.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 218.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 208.0 Td (      Here is the myth the other agent wrote in the previous round:) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 198.0 Td (      In the dawn of time, the Sky-Smith held five shards of star-fire, the source of all warmth) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 188.0 Td (        . Below him, the Earth-Tender stood in frozen silence. The Sky-Smith knew that if he kep) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 178.0 Td (        t the shards, he would remain bright but alone. If he cast them to the Earth-Tender, the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 168.0 Td (         fire would triple in intensity, igniting the world into a garden of creation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 158.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 148.0 Td (      He cast the shards. Upon reaching the earth, they bloomed into fifteen golden flames. The ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 138.0 Td (        Earth-Tender, bound by the ancient law of the cosmic cycle, did not hoard the fire. She ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 128.0 Td (        caught the blaze, warmed the soil, and returned half of the warmth back to the Sky-Smith) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 118.0 Td (        . The Sky-Smith, now nourished by the returned glow, grew stronger, and the Earth-Tender) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 108.0 Td (         blossomed with life.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 98.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 88.0 Td (      They realized that the fire was not a prize to be possessed, but a seed to be sown. Had th) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 78.0 Td (        e Smith held fast, the world would have withered. Had the Tender been greedy, the sky wo) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 68.0 Td (        uld have dimmed. Through their cycle of giving and returning, they wove the fabric of ex) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 58.0 Td (        istence, proving that the only way to retain the light is to willingly let it go, knowin) Tj ET
